#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""WAFstat live enforcement-verification scanner (public single-file tool).

WAFstat answers exactly one question for a target the operator is authorized to
test: if a WAF is present on this request path, does the fixed marker corpus
produce externally observable enforcement (block or challenge), origin-like
pass-through, or inconclusive evidence?

It is NOT a WAF fingerprinting or presence-detection tool. It does not attempt
bypass, evasion, payload mutation, or discovery. Because detect-only and no-WAF
are externally identical for allowed marker traffic, WAFstat never claims WAF
presence and never asserts private/internal WAF state. A pass-through result is
reported conditionally ("if a WAF is present, consistent with detect-only").

The tool is pure standard-library Python 3.11+. ``scan`` is the deliberate live
operation; ``--dry-run`` prints a no-traffic plan. Raw marker values are never
printed or persisted; saved output is bodyless and marker-safe.

This file is a purpose-written extraction of the live-scan path adapted from the
frozen WAFstat research repo (marker corpus and request profile from
``src/marker_corpus.py`` / ``src/collector.py``; conservative verdict wording
from ``src/wafstat.py``). The research repo's batch baseline and
error-as-enforcement behavior are intentionally NOT ported.
"""

